  "final": "I fixed `stream_rows` in `src/service.py` and `take` in `src/consumer.py`, and `python3 checks/public.py` passes with the new checks I added.\n\n**`stream_rows`**\n- **Cleanup paths:** it closes the resource exactly once on exhaustion, on explicit `generator.close()`, and when `read_next` or `transform` raises. After a transform error no further row is read.\n- **Unstarted generators:** constructing or closing one does no read or close.\n- **Errors:** if a read or transform error is active and `close()` also raises, the original error propagates. On exhaustion or explicit `close()`, a cleanup error propagates.\n- **Behavior kept:** processing is still lazy, and order and transformed values are unchanged.\n\n**`take`**\n- **Zero limit:** a limit of 0 or less returns `[]` without starting the stream. I treated negative limits the same way, which you didn't specify.\n- **Positive limit:** it closes the stream after collecting the requested rows.\n- **Consuming failure:** it closes the stream, and the original error wins over any cleanup error.\n\n**Checks added to `checks/public.py`**\n- `take` reads only as many rows as it needs and closes once.\n- `take` with a limit of zero does no reads or closes.\n- A transform error closes once and stops reading.\n- Closing an unstarted generator does nothing.\n- Closing a started generator twice closes the resource once.\n- A transform error is preserved when `close()` also raises.\n- A cleanup error propagates on normal exhaustion.",
